[node name="ForestFog" parent="ForestMap"]
editor_description = "The pit's mist, and the reason the pit has no bottom: fourteen layers y -11.6..-5.75, grey-gold, darker and greyer low (maps/forest/materials/forest_mist.gdshader, unshaded: a 64 px noise tile drifting at its own speed and heading per layer lightens, darkens and thins each layer, never under 0.6 of its vertex alpha; each layer fades out over 1 m of depth and darkens over 6 m where it meets bank, trunk or thorn, so the mist has no lit rim), opaque (vertex alpha 1.0; the proof assumes the shader draws at least 0.6 of it, faded as the shader fades it over the floor) through the bottom TEN, then falling to exactly 0 over the top ~1.35 m at a height the drift field rolls (no top plane anywhere), with the rim feather narrowing from 9 m up top to nothing through the slab (its body reaches the bank's foot, the feather is in the earth) and the hole round the trunk closed to 0.6 m. Transmittance -- the fraction of the floor's light reaching an eye -- is 0.20 % straight down and at worst 0.20 % along any oblique ray from a stand point (lip edge, lane, guard's floor and rim -- forest_pit_build.FOG_EYES), so the floor is not a thing anyone sees: what they see is mist, and the brambles sink into it as dark bark (COLOR_0, forest_pit_build.stem_colour: bark the whole stem, no blend toward the mist -- that washed the near thorns; the opaque fog hides them under its top). fog_disabled in the shader is load-bearing: without it the Environment's depth fog repaints these layers AND the floor behind them to one colour, and one colour over one flat plane is a floor. Run tools/modelling/maps/forest/forest_pit_build.py for the numbers. The stack's ceiling is set by the THICKET, not by the pit: the tallest thorn stands at y -2.78, and a stack that ended at y 4.0 buried the thicket whole -- not one bramble vertex cleared even the thinnest column. The top rolls between y -7.80 and y -5.75, so the brambles come OUT of the mist. Environment height fog moved with it (fog_height -5.75, fog_height_density 0.1): Godot thickens height fog BELOW fog_height, so leaving it at 8 would have left haze standing in the 13 m of ravine air the fog just vacated and washed out the very thorn tips this pass uncovered. Above y -5.75 the ravine air is clear and the spikes read hard; below it the haze thickens into the layers."
surface_material_override/0 = SubResource("FogMat")

